e it clear to  william that regardless of what he did  in the future he definitely did not want  to live the rest of his life in the  village herding cattle and keeping  records so william started focusing on  leaving the village and seeing the  outside world and his first major  opportunity to do so was of course  college william attended peaking  university in beijing which is actually  one of the most prestigious universities  in china  apparently it's the center of  humanity-based studies in china and is  often called the harvard of china  currently their acceptance rate sits at  a measly 0.91 percent so given that  william got accepted he clearly knew  what he was doing anyway william majored  in sociology and one of the first things  he did after enrolling was pick up a job  as a deputy monitor at the university  his responsibilities included leading  trips to historic places like the great  wall of china and helping his cohort  build teamwork skills william had no  complaints with the job but  unfortunately he would fall ill and be  hospitalized for a month and by the time  he recovered he lost the job this  experience made william realize how  dispensable he was and in an effort to  combat this he would try to add a few  specialties to his skill set this  included getting a minor in computer  science and the law soon enough william  graduated from college but he was still  as lost as ever before so he decided to  follow what interested him  one of william's biggest passions was  cars but william wasn't ready to do  anything nearly as ambitious as starting  up an eevee company so he gauged the  business landscape and the most obvious  opportunity he saw was the internet so  in 1996 william decided to combine his  passion for cars and the opportunity  that came with the internet in order to  find a precursor to bid auto today bit  auto has evolved into being one of the  largest automotive publications in china  and they do everything from marketing  consultations to providing transactional  services but back in 1996 bit auto was  basically just a blog and william was  the main reporter given the rapid growth  of the internet throughout the late  1990s and the lack of chinese automotive  content at the time william was able to  build up a solid following rather  quickly which allowed him to go from  barely making ends meet to being pretty  well off despite the evolution of his  finances though william had no urge to  stop being a reporter and take on a more  traditional role as a ceo he says that  being a reporter gave him insight into  the automotive industry that he couldn't  get anywhere else and it wasn't until  2000 that williams stepped back from  reporting and officially founded bit  auto holdings while william was  initially hesitant to make the move once  he made the move he was fully committed  he split the business up into three  segments which included the advertising  and subscription business the  transaction services business and the  digital marketing solutions business  starting with this advertising business  this is basically what he was already  doing he sold advertising spots on his  news portal to automakers dealerships  and insurance companies given that all  of you guys are watching youtube i'm  sure you all know how this works moving  on to their transactions business bit  auto partnered with the leading  automobile finance platform in china for  the yixing group limited to provide  various financial services to their  customers and finally in terms of the  digital marketing business given that  bit auto had achieved moderate success  on the internet williams started to  provide digital marketing consulting  services to automakers and dealerships  you know the classic how to set up a  website how to drive traffic to the site  how to convert viewers into customers  and so on and so forth william did this  for the next 13 years which not only  made him wealthy but gave him  significant connections within the  automotive world and this put him in the  perfect position to start up neo  being involved in the automotive world  for decades at this point it was clear  to william that evs were the future he  wasn't the only one that was convinced  of this future either basically all of  corporate china and the government were  confident that evs were the future and  given the pollution problem in china  they wanted to switch over as soon as  possible as a result when william  proposed the idea of founding an eb  startup dozens of high-flying tech  companies lined up to provide funding  this included tensioned xiaomi sequoia  capital lenovo temesec and tpg and with  that william founded neo in november of  2014 but it wasn't originally called a  neo initially the company didn't really  have a name and they were simply  referred to it as nextdv  anyway despite all this funding and the  involvement of big players the general  public had no clue what nextdb was given  that nextdb vehicles were consumer  vehicles this wasn't a good thing so  william's first order of business was to  generate hype around the brand and what  better way to do that than show off a  flashy sports car in november of 2016  next tv debuted the neo ep9 sports car  the car would actually become the most  expensive ev ever built clocking in at  3.48 million dollars the ep9 was also  the fastest ev ever built with a maximum  speed of 217 miles per hour despite the  impressive stats this car was obviously  not reasonable for the average person in  china but this didn't matter  nyo had no intentions of mass producing  this car  this was simply a publicity stunt like  the tesla roadster to generate hype and  that's exactly what it accomplished soon  enough people from around the world were  talking about newest crazy electric  sports car that could break 200 miles  per hour and this gave neo the perfect  opportunity to launch the luxury suv the  es-8 in late 2017. the es8 wasn't  exactly cheap but it also wasn't crazy  expensive either it started at 67 000  which made it comfortably affordable to  well-off families initially neo's  customers and car magazines loved the  es8 but unfortunately this didn't last  too long  you see it starting in late 2018 stories  of es-8s catching on fire start to  surface there were only a handful of  cases but as you would guess this was  terrible for nearest public reputation  neo would end up recalling the esi in  2019 and in the year's delivery numbers  would subsequently jump off a cliff in  late 2018 neo was delivering over 3000  es8s per month but by mid-2019 they were  delivering less than 500 es8s per month  neo tried to combat this decline by  launching a smaller suv called the es6  and this did help quite a bit especially  given the lower starting price but neo  was still in a pretty rough position  financially and reputation wise at the  end of 2019  out of nowhere though tesla would reveal  the cyber truck and tesla stock would go  to the moon and the rest is history  even though new stock was sent to the  moon along with tesla from a low of one  dollar to nearly 70  neo was far from safe due to three main  reasons starting with their investor  base  most of the people who invested in neo  did not invest because they were  confident in their financials or  optimistic about their future in most  cases they missed out on tesla and they  didn't want to buy into tesla at several  hundred dollars so they looked for the  next tesla and the most obvious choice  within the stock market was neo  considering this a significant portion  of neo's investors were short-term  investors who are looking to make a  quick buck so you can bet that these  guys left as soon as neo started to dip  substantially which has made it  significantly harder to do share  offerings and raise money and that  brings us into new second major problem  which is that they're not profitable nor  are they close to being profitable most  of tesla's run occurred right after  tesla achieved profitability after years  of struggling with model 3 production  in fact tesla needed bankruptcy on  several occasions while trying to ramp  up production  well i mean tesla really faced the  severe  uh threat of death uh due to the model 3  production ramp  essentially the company was bleeding  money like crazy and and just  if if we didn't solve these problems in  a very short period of time uh we would  die uh and it was extremely difficult  to solve them how close to death did you  come we are within  single budget weeks and this is exactly  where neo is at right now last year they  delivered 91 000 vehicles and over the  next few years they have to scale to  hundreds of thousands if we take a look  back at 2017 tesla delivered 103 000  cars and over the next few years they  scaled to hundreds of thousands which is  when they faced the vast majority of  their financial problems so there's no  doubt that the next few years will be  extremely difficult for neo given that  they're just now entering production  hell and finally moving on to the last  major factor weighing down neo we have  the chinese government now the chinese  government has no problems with neo  specifically in fact they're major  proponents of the eevee revolution  however they've been fighting a  corporate war against jack ma and the  listing were against the us for the past  18 months and all this uncertainty has  basically crashed the entire chinese  stock market in fact alibaba nearly  revisited ipo levels after dropping 76  percent from their all-time high so  antonio sheds all of its short-term  investors survived production hell and  the chinese government lightens up neo's  future remains uncertain  despite all this uncertainty personally  i don't think neo is going anywhere for  one reason which is the chinese  government while their actions thus far  have been inadvertently hurting neo i'm  rather confident that they won't let neo  go bankrupt for the ccp saving neo is  not only a matter of reducing pollution  within china but a matter of national  pride china really wants to beat the  rest of the world to evs and to be the  first world power to make the transition  and sacrificing one of their biggest ev  producers definitely won't help that  also we should note that the ccp has  helped neo in the past in late 2019 when  nia was facing the recall crisis the ccp  gave them a one billion dollar bailout  at the time neo's entire market cap was  hovering at just one to two billion  dollars so the ccp basically gave them a  loan that was the size of their market  cap today neo is worth over 30 billion  dollars even with the recent decline so  i think the ccp would be more willing  than ever to loan them a couple billion  if needed personally i wouldn't invest  in neo because i'd rather just invest in  tesla but with that being said given ccp  support i don't think neo is going  anywhere anytime soon despite the  horrendous balance sheet but that's just  what i think do you guys think neo will  go bankrupt comment that down below  also drop a like if you hope neo  survives and accelerates ev adoption and  of course consider checking out our  international channels to watch the  videos in other languages and consider  subscribing to see more questions  logically answered but until then i'm  hari and i'll see you guys on the next  one  [Music]
 

